  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 300 PM EDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nCurrently-Tonight... KMLB radar imagery shows general slow moving\nwest-northwest moving scattered showers and lightning storms\nacross east central Florida. GOES-19 satellite imagery and local\nobservations indicate partly to mostly cloudy skies. Temperatures\nare currently in the mid 80s to low 90s with heat index values\ngenerally between 100F-108F degrees. Dew points are in the mid 70s\nto around 80 degrees. Analysis charts show the western fringe of \nthe Bermuda high over local Atlantic waters. The 15Z XMR sounding\nindicates MLCAPE at 2,455 J/kg, PWAT at 2.2\", and 0-6km shear at\n11 kts. Scattered to numerous showers and lightning storms are \nforecast to increase in coverage into the late afternoon and \nevening as collisions occur between easterly flow, the west coast \nsea breeze over western central Florida, as well as outflow from \nprevious storms. Rain chances are forecast to gradually diminish  \naround 9PM. Isolated showers and lightning storms cannot be ruled \nout from Cape Canaveral southwards. The potential exists for a \ncouple to a a few strong storms capable of frequent lightning, \nwind gusts up to 40-50mph, and heavy rainfall (1-3\"+ in 60 \nminutes). Above normal (~1-3F+) lows in the low to upper 70s.\n\nFriday-Saturday... The Atlantic ridge is expected to continue to \nbuild over the western Atlantic into the weekend. A more typical\ndiurnally driven sea breeze pattern is expected compared to the\npast several days which will result in a gradual increase in rain\nchances at the coast with offshore moving showers and lightning\nstorms. Scattered to numerous showers (~50-70% chance) and \nlightning storms are forecast to develop each day (mainly between\n12PM-8PM) across east central Florida as collisions occur between\nsea breezes and outflow from previous storms. The greatest \ncoverage is forecast generally along and near I-4 where the \ngreatest surface convergence is forecast. Guidance continues to\nindicate an environment sufficient for isolated strong storms \nwith high instability, weak bulk shear, and plentiful moisture. \nThe strong storm potential will be greatest on Friday with \nguidance showing PVA pivoting across east central Florida which \nwill provide additional lift for strong to marginally severe \nstorm(s).\n\nThe primary hazards for a couple to a few strong storms on Friday\nand Saturday are frequent to excessive lightning, wind gusts up \nto 40-50mph (<5% chance of wind gusts up to 60mph on Friday), and \nheavy rainfall (up to 1-3\"+ in 60 minutes). Afternoon highs in the\nupper 80s to low 90s with heat index values up to 102F-107F are \nforecast. Above normal lows (~1-4+ degrees) in the low to upper \n70s are forecast. There is a Moderate HeatRisk with areas of \nMajor HeatRisk on Friday and Saturday. A Moderate HeatRisk \naffects those sensitive to heat and a Major HeatRisk affects \nanyone without adequate cooling and hydration. \n\nSunday-Wednesday... Global models and LREF-clusters are in good \nagreement that a mid/upper level high will build over the eastern \nCONUS Sunday and into next week. Thus, a continuation of the \ndiurnally sea breeze showers and lightning storm pattern is \nforecast through Monday before onshore flow develops across east \ncentral Florida and strengthens Tuesday into Wednesday. Scattered \nto numerous/widespread showers (50-90% chance Sunday reduce to ) \nand lightning storms are forecast each afternoon and evening. \nGlobal models and the NBM indicate that rain chances are forecast\nto decrease into next week as onshore flow becomes established \nover east central Florida. Global models continue to show high \ninstability with weak shear and sufficient moisture during the\nforecast period which is conducive for isolated to scattered \nstrong storms capable of frequent lightning, strong wind gusts, \nand heavy rainfall. There is a up to 20-70% chance of a Major \nHeatRisk for areas of east central Florida Sunday to Monday with \nlower chances on Tuesday (up to 20-50% chance).",
